% ============================================================================
% author-purpose — the three classic reasons an author writes something and
% what each actually is (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Every piece of writing is written for a REASON — the classic "PIE" trio
% taught in reading comprehension: to persuade the reader toward a view, to
% inform the reader of real-world facts, or to entertain the reader with an
% engaging read. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? author_purpose(persuade, $D)
%       % convince_the_reader_of_the_merits_of_a_particular_point_of_view
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `author_purpose(purpose, description)` carrying the source citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns
% the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on an author-purpose-related term that is not one of these
% three. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the
% purpose it names:
%
%     ? author_purpose($P, enlighten_the_readership_about_a_real_world_topic)
%       % inform
%
% The three purposes, as a little truth table:
%
%     purpose      description                                                          quote (verbatim)
%     -----------  -------------------------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     persuade      convince_the_reader_of_the_merits_of_a_particular_point_of_view       "When a text is written to persuade, it aims to convince the reader of the merits of a particular point of view."
%     inform        enlighten_the_readership_about_a_real_world_topic                     "When an author aims to inform, they usually wish to enlighten their readership about a real-world topic."
%     entertain     keep_things_as_interesting_as_possible                                "When an author's chief purpose is to entertain the reader, they will endeavour to keep things as interesting as possible."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from LiteracyIdeas' "The Author's Purpose:
% Ultimate Guide for Teachers and Students" article, which introduces each
% of these three under its own "To ___" heading with a single, parallel,
% "when an author's purpose is to X, they Y" defining sentence. This is a
% MULTI-SOURCE-STYLE table in the sense established by `point-of-view.adj`/
% `comma-rule.adj`/`figurative-language-type.adj` (one article, one clean
% sentence per named section) — this `table`'s own `source` field carries
% the first row's (persuade) quote; the other two rows' quotes are
% documented above in this header's truth table and are independently
% checkable against the same `locator`. WebFetch-verified across two
% passes before writing, the second pass pulling the full first paragraph
% under each heading to confirm the target sentence stands alone as a
% complete, single-fact definition and isn't itself the FIRST of two
% sentences it depends on (the persuade section's first sentence, "This is
% a prevalent purpose of writing, particularly in nonfiction," is a
% separate prevalence claim the quoted sentence does not depend on).
% `trust consensus` — a reputable secondary literacy-education reference,
% the same tier this stdlib already reserves for its other non-Grammarly
% teaching-site citations.
%
% A fourth purpose the SAME article also names under its own "To Describe"
% heading is deliberately NOT a row here, so a recall ABSTAINS on it
% rather than inventing a description: `describe`. It is a real purpose
% category the source covers, but its defining sentence ("Writers often
% use words to describe something in more detail than conveyed in a
% photograph alone") is framed as a comparison to photography rather than
% the same parallel "when an author's purpose is to X, they Y" pattern the
% three tabled here share, and the classic PIE mnemonic this table grounds
% names only these three as the canonical peer set. Picked using the
% mandatory full-tree-grep-before-scoping discipline — zero hits for
% "author_purpose"/"persuade"/"entertain" before this file was written,
% after checking 12 not-yet-reviewed literacy tables this window
% (clause-type.adj, comma-rule.adj, point-of-view.adj, sound-device-type.adj,
% figurative-language-type.adj, sentence-type.adj, idiom-meaning.adj,
% simile-meaning.adj, past-tense-ed-sound.adj, plural-s-sound.adj,
% silent-e-word.adj, r-controlled-vowel-word.adj — none extendable, each
% already documenting in its own header exactly why its rejected
% candidates don't qualify). (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table author_purpose {
    columns purpose, description

    row (persuade, convince_the_reader_of_the_merits_of_a_particular_point_of_view)
    row (inform, enlighten_the_readership_about_a_real_world_topic)
    row (entertain, keep_things_as_interesting_as_possible)

    source "When a text is written to persuade, it aims to convince the reader of the merits of a particular point of view."
    locator "https://literacyideas.com/authors-purpose/"
    trust consensus
}
